    "problem_statement": "# Adding a column to an empty table raises ValueError incorrectly\n\n## Description\n\nWhen trying to add a column to an empty table (with no rows), PrettyTable incorrectly raises a ValueError. This happens because the condition for validating column length is inverted.\n\nHere's a simple reproduction:\n\n```python\nfrom prettytable import PrettyTable\n\n# Create an empty table\ntable = PrettyTable()\n\n# Try to add a column - this should work but fails\ntable.add_column(\"Column 1\", [\"Value 1\"])\n```\n\nThe error message is:\n```\nValueError: Column length 1 does not match number of rows 0\n```\n\nThis is unexpected behavior because adding a column to an empty table should be allowed. The table should automatically create the necessary rows to accommodate the column data.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nWhen adding a column to an empty table, the table should create the necessary rows to accommodate the column data, rather than raising an error.\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nAdding a column to an empty table raises a ValueError stating that the column length doesn't match the number of rows, even though there are no rows yet.\n\nThis issue also affects the ability to build tables using the column-by-column approach when starting with an empty table."
  },
